<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="dTMP de novo biosynthesis (mitochondrial)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Cellular dTTP levels need to be tightly controlled for efficient DNA replication in the cell nucleus and mitochondria |CITS: [9148948]|. An imbalance of cellular dTTP results in the incorporation of uracil into DNA which can cause instability in the nuclear genome and mitochondrial dysfunction |CITS: [21876188]|. The nucleus has been shown to possess both a salvage (|FRAME: PWY-7199|) and a &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; (|FRAME: PWY-7184|) pathway for dTTP synthesis via dTMP. The salvage pathway also occurs in the mitchondria |CITS: [14519855]|. However, dTTP requirements for mitochondrial DNA replication cannot be sustained by the salvage pathway alone. Studies to elucidate how mitochondria maintained their dTTP levels led to the discovery of a novel &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; dTMP biosynthetic pathway unique to mitochondria |CITS: [21876188]|. &lt;b&gt;About this Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Two of the enzymes in this pathway, |FRAME: HS11096-MONOMER| and |FRAME: HS00049-MONOMER| (SHMT2) were known to be localized to the mitochondria in mammalian cells. The third enzyme |FRAME: ENSG00000178700-MONOMER| was initially considered to be a pseudogene |CITS: [3341383]|. It was later revealed as a fully functional isozyme of |FRAME: CPLX66-351|, unique to mitochondria |CITS: [21876188]|. Folates like tetrahydrofolate and tetrahydrofolate glutamate are essential cofactors for the pathway. They mediate the one carbon transfers necessary for the synthesis of dTMP. They are also required for purines, methionine and glycine biosynthesis |CITS: [14519855]|. Folate deficiency is associated with increased uracil misincorporation into DNA |CITS: [9096386]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="AU4FS-4" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
